WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.185 --> 00:00:10.426
Priya Raman: Hi Rosa, hi Heather. Thanks for making time this afternoon. How's the week going out there?

2
00:00:11.301 --> 00:00:19.024
Heather Collins: Pretty good. Busy, but that's normal for October. We've got two offices short a hygienist, so everyone's picking up extra days.

3
00:00:20.458 --> 00:00:32.274
Rosa Jimenez: Hillcrest is one of them. I've been covering front desk two afternoons a week, which is why I'm on this call from the back office instead of my desk.

4
00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:40.224
Priya Raman: That sounds like a lot. Do you want to keep this short, or is now still okay?

5
00:00:41.328 --> 00:00:46.736
Rosa Jimenez: No, now works. I just might step away if a patient walks up.

6
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:57.332
Priya Raman: Understood. Before we get into anything, I pulled the numbers for September. Cedar Ridge tracked a little over 11,400 calls across the 19 practices.

7
00:00:57.332 --> 00:01:01.857
Priya Raman: About 3,100 of those came in on the Google Ads numbers.

8
00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:10.998
Heather Collins: That tracks with what I saw. New-patient calls were up maybe eight percent month over month, mostly from the Boise and Meridian campaigns.

9
00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:18.265
Priya Raman: Good. And the offline conversion uploads to Google Ads ran clean all month, no rejected matches that I saw.

10
00:01:18.623 --> 00:01:25.339
Heather Collins: Yeah, that side has been quiet. We haven't touched the tag or the number pools since spring.

11
00:01:26.024 --> 00:01:32.783
Priya Raman: How's the daily routine on your end, Rosa? I know the practice managers live in the summaries.

12
00:01:34.054 --> 00:01:53.141
Rosa Jimenez: We do. I read them every morning before the huddle. Honestly, the summaries have been really good for us. My front desk and the treatment coordinators say they save a ton of time on callbacks, because they can see what the patient actually wanted without listening to the whole recording.

13
00:01:53.912 --> 00:01:57.196
Priya Raman: So it's holding up across the team, not just you?

14
00:01:57.914 --> 00:02:07.828
Rosa Jimenez: Right. Even when the call gets handed off, like front desk to scheduling to the back, whoever picks it up knows where it left off.

15
00:02:07.828 --> 00:02:13.777
Rosa Jimenez: The summary is accurate enough that they trust it. That's the part I didn't expect.

16
00:02:14.445 --> 00:02:23.421
Priya Raman: That's good to hear. Do you mind if I pass that along internally? It's useful for the team that works on summaries.

17
00:02:24.307 --> 00:02:28.325
Rosa Jimenez: Sure, go ahead. Just don't put my name on a slide.

18
00:02:28.616 --> 00:02:36.413
Priya Raman: I'll keep it general. Heather, anything on the operations side you want to cover while we have the time?

19
00:02:37.561 --> 00:02:53.826
Heather Collins: One thing. We're bringing the Eagle office onto the same call flow as the others in November. Right now they're still on the old routing, so their calls don't roll to the shared front desk after hours.

20
00:02:54.284 --> 00:02:57.158
Priya Raman: Okay. Who's doing that work on your side?

21
00:02:58.607 --> 00:03:08.005
Heather Collins: Dana's team handles the phone system, and I'll own the Invoca side. I mostly need to know what I have to change versus what stays put.

22
00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:23.728
Priya Raman: Let me look at their setup and send you a short list. If it's just pointing the Eagle number at the existing flow, it should be quick, but I don't want to guess until I've seen it.

23
00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:29.136
Heather Collins: That works. No rush, we're targeting the first week of November.

24
00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:36.066
Priya Raman: I'll get it to you by Monday. Rosa, how are the new hires doing on the phones?

25
00:03:37.337 --> 00:03:48.104
Rosa Jimenez: Two of them started in September. They're getting there. The first couple weeks they were marking calls by hand, and now they mostly let the signals do it.

26
00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:55.519
Priya Raman: Are the signals catching what they expect? Appointment booked, quote given, that kind of thing.

27
00:03:55.917 --> 00:04:00.692
Rosa Jimenez: From what they tell me, yes. Nobody's complained. It's been fine.

28
00:04:00.901 --> 00:04:04.027
Priya Raman: Good. Anything on the Meta side worth flagging?

29
00:04:04.975 --> 00:04:16.320
Heather Collins: Nothing new. We kept the same campaign structure through the fall. Our agency asked for a call volume export last month and got it without me having to get involved.

30
00:04:16.544 --> 00:04:25.275
Priya Raman: Nice. Okay, one scheduling note. Your renewal is next April, and I usually start that conversation in January. Does that still work for you?

31
00:04:25.786 --> 00:04:30.514
Heather Collins: January's fine. Nothing's changing on our end that I know of.

32
00:04:31.677 --> 00:04:39.391
Priya Raman: Great. I'll also send a short recap of today, mostly the Eagle timeline, so you have it in writing.

33
00:04:40.375 --> 00:04:47.233
Heather Collins: Please do. And if you need anything from us for the list, just email me directly.

34
00:04:47.597 --> 00:04:55.021
Rosa Jimenez: Same here. I'm slower on email when I'm covering the desk, but I'll get back to you.

35
00:04:55.767 --> 00:05:02.407
Priya Raman: No problem at all. Thanks for the time, both of you. Hang in there with the staffing.

36
00:05:03.817 --> 00:05:05.471
Rosa Jimenez: Thanks, Priya. Talk soon.

37
00:05:06.587 --> 00:05:07.778
Heather Collins: Thanks. Bye now.
